  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 222 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nNext Pacific storm system is on tap to move across the region\nthrough the course of the weekend, bringing mountain snow and\ncooler temperatures. The aforementioned system will move into \nthe US as a closed system, but by the time it moves into our \nneck of the woods should become an open trough. A second, weaker\nsystem may drop into the region from Canada and combine with \nthe overall flow on Saturday, with these features then \nprogressing by Sunday as a transient ridge then becomes the \ndominant feature to start next week. By Tuesday though our next \nsystem will move into the region to end the forecast period. For\nFriday into Saturday we're still on track for some snowfall to \nmove up with the Pacific system, but with the strongest \nmoisture remaining to our south and east alongside the best \nforcing, the bulk of this activity should be relegated to \norographic lift across the mountains, though some light snow \noverspreading into the nearby lower elevation zones and valleys \ncould be possible, and on Saturday we could even see some \nprecipitation moving into the I-80 corridor from Cheyenne \nthrough Sidney. Also on Friday some meager instability will be \npresent west of the Laramie Range which could promote some snow \nsquall activity. Colder temperatures also accompany these \nsystems, and we'll see the warmer than average highs from \nThursday and Friday plummeting by Saturday with highs around 5 \ndegrees below normal, placing the region in the 30's to 40's. \nSunday we'll dry out under the transient ridge, but by early \nnext week the incoming system brings another chance at \nwidespread precipitation mainly in the form of light snow. But \nwith how precipitation chances have been evaporating as we get \nwithin 1 to 3 days of the event, don't be surprised if once \nagain the mountains are the only location to see any noteworthy \naccumulations by the time we hit next week. Overall there is \nmoderate to high confidence in the forecast with the only true \nvariations in ensembles showing towards the end of the period \nbetween the snowfall expectations early next week as well as the\nstrength of the features as we move into the middle of next \nweek.",
